lesson.example:
Imagine a company called 'Target Example' uses a private tool named 'target-secret-login'. In their public GitHub, they have a file called package.json:
{
  "dependencies": {
    "target-secret-login": "1.0.0"
  }
}
An attacker sees this and goes to npmjs.com (a public registry). They see 'target-secret-login' is not taken. They register it and upload a version labeled '2.0.0'. The next time Target Example's servers update, they see version 2.0.0 on the internet and download the attacker's code instead of their own version 1.0.0.

lesson.fix:
To prevent this, companies should use 'Scoped Packages' (e.g., @hartwell/package-name) and reserve that scope on public registries so no one else can use it. Additionally, build systems should be configured to use 'lockfiles' (like package-lock.json) which record the exact source and hash (a unique digital fingerprint) of every package to ensure it hasn't changed. Finally, internal registries should be prioritized over public ones in the system configuration.

Example of a scoped package in package.json:
{
  "dependencies": {
    "@hartwell/internal-tool": "1.0.0"
  }
}
